<section xml:id="sec-ch05-01-VectorFieldsAndLineIntegrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Vector Fields and Line Integrals</title>

  <note>
    <p>So far, we have studied two types of functions:</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Vector-valued functions (one input variable, multiple output
            variables) of the form
            <md>
              \vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Scalar-valued functions (multiple input variables, one output
            variable) of the form
            <md>
              f(x, y)=z
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, we will look at functions of multiple input variables and
      multiple output variables.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Vector Fields in <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m></title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A function of the form
        <m>\vec{F}(x, y)=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> is a vector
        field in the <m>x y</m>-plane.
      </p>

      <p>
        The vector field <m>\vec{F}</m> assigns to each point
        ( <m>x, y</m> ) in the <m>x y</m>-plane, a vector <m>\vec{F}(x, y)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      With vector fields, we don't put the vectors back into standard
      position (since the starting location <m>(x, y)</m> of each vector
      gives us important information).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-vector-fields">
    <caption><m>\vec F = \langle 1-y,0 \rangle</m> (left) and <m>\vec F = \langle -y,-x \rangle</m> (right)</caption>

