  <definition>
    <title>Vector Fields in <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}</m></title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A function of the form
        <m>\mathrm{F}(x, y, z)=\langle f(x, y, z), g(x, y, z), h(x, y, z)\rangle</m>
        is a vector field in <m>x y z</m>-space.
      </p>

      <p>
        The vector field <m>\vec{F}</m> assigns to each point
        ( <m>x, y, z</m> ) in <m>x y z</m>-space, a vector
        <m>\vec{F}(x, y, z)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      Suppose that an electron is moving in a magnetic field. If it is
      moving in the direction of the field, then it accelerates (energy is
      being transferred to the electron). How can we measure that energy
      transfer?
    </p>

    <p>
      We will start by deriving a way to integrate a vector field
      <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> over a 2D curve
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle</m> in its domain. We will
      call such an integral a vector field line integral.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Derivation of a Vector Field Line Integral</title>

    <p>
      Suppose you start with an oriented curve <m>C</m> parametrized as
      <m>\vec{r}(t)= \langle x(t), y(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>, and
      with a vector field <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m>. We
      follow a similar process as with scalar line integrals.
    </p>

    <p>
      We start by partitioning <m>C</m> into <m>n</m> pieces of arc length
      <m>\Delta s_{k}</m>. Second, we pick a point ( <m>x_{k}, y_{k}</m> )
      in the <m>k</m>-th segment of <m>C</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, when computing scalar line integrals, if we were integrating a
      surface <m>h(x, y)</m>, we would multiply <m>\Delta s_{k}</m> by
      <m>h\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m>. When integrating a vector field,
      we can't do this since <m>\vec{F}\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m> is a
      vector, not a number. Hence, we instead use the quantity
      <m>\vec{F}_{k} \cdot \vec{T}_{k}</m>, where
      <m>\vec{F}_{k}=\vec{F}\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m> and
      <m>\vec{T}_{k}</m> is the unit tangent vector to
      <m>C \left(\frac{\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)}{\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right|}\right)</m>
      at the point <m>\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right)</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-projection">
      <caption>From OpenStax</caption>

      <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-02.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>The projection of a vector of the vector field onto the tangent vector of a curve.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>The projection of a vector of the vector field onto the tangent vector of a curve.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Finally, we add these quantities up and take the limit as the number
      of segments <m>n</m> approaches infinity to get
      <md>
        \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} \vec{F}_{k} \cdot \vec{T}_{k} \Delta s_{k}=\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Important: We can interpret
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> as adding up the scalar
      component of <m>\vec{F}</m> (the vector field) in the direction of
      <m>\vec{T}</m> (the curve) at each point along <m>C</m>.</alert>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Evaluating a Vector Field Line Integral</title>

    <p>
      We can find a nice computational formula for
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> by noting that
      <md>
        \vec{T}=\frac{\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)}{\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right|}, \quad ds = \left|\vec{r}' (t) \right|dt
      </md>
      and hence
      <md>
        \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\left(\vec{F} \cdot \frac{\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)}{\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right|}\right) \left|\vec{r}' (t) \right|dt=\vec{F} \cdot \vec r'(t) d t
      </md>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Vector Field Line Integral</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>C</m> be a smooth curve parametrized by <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>,
      <m>a \leq t \leq b</m> and <m>\vec{F}</m> be a (continuous) vector
      field over a region containing <m>C</m>. Then the vector field line
      integral of <m>\vec{F}</m> over <m>C</m> is evaluated as
      <md>
        \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=\int_{a}^{b} \vec{F}(\vec{r}(t)) \cdot \vec{r}^{\prime}(t) d t
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> where
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle y, -x\rangle</m> on the following oriented
        curves in the plane:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) The quarter-circle <m>C_{1}</m> from <m>P(1,0)</m> to
        <m>Q(0,1)</m> (in black).
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) The quarter-circle - <m>C_{1}</m> from <m>Q(0,1)</m> to
        <m>P(1,0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (c) The path <m>C_{2}</m> from <m>Q(0,1)</m> to <m>P(1,0)</m> via
        two line segments through <m>O(0,0)</m> (in red).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-3">
        <caption>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the x and y axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-04. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-04.png">
              <shortdescription>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the x and y axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>C_1 : \vec r (t) = \langle \cos t , \sin t \rangle , \  0\leq t \leq \frac{\pi}{2}, \quad \vec r ' (t) = \langle -\sin t , \cos t \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) = \langle \sin t, - \cos t \rangle , \quad \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) = -\sin^2 t + (-\cos^2 t) = -1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C_1} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_1} \vec F\ d\vec r =\int_a^b \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}} -1 \ dt  =-\frac{\pi}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        b)
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>-C_1 : \vec r (t) = \langle \sin t , \cos t \rangle , \  0\leq t \leq \frac{\pi}{2}, \quad \vec r ' (t) = \langle -\cos t , -\sin t \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) = \langle \cos t, - \sin t \rangle , \quad \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) = \cos^2 t + \sin^2 t = 1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C_1} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_1} \vec F\ d\vec r =\int_a^b \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{2}} 1 \ dt  =\frac{\pi}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        c)
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\text{Line in space: }\vec l (t) = \vec v + t \vec u ,</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{ where }\vec v\text{ represents the starting point and }\vec u\text{ is a vector in the direction of the line.}</mrow>
          <mrow>C_2 : \vec r (t) = \begin{cases} \langle 0,1 \rangle + t \langle 0 , -1 \rangle = \langle 0,1-t \rangle\amp\quad 0\leq t \leq 1\\ \langle 0,0 \rangle + (t-1) \langle 1 , 0 \rangle = \langle t-1, 0 \rangle\amp\quad 1\leq t \leq 2\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r' (t) =   \begin{cases} \langle 0,-1 \rangle\amp\quad 0\lt  t \lt 1\\ \langle 1, 0 \rangle\amp\quad 1\lt t \lt 2\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t))  =   \begin{cases} \langle 1-t , 0\rangle\amp\quad 0\leq  t \leq 1\\ \langle 0, -t+1 \rangle\amp\quad 1\leq t \leq 2\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) =  \begin{cases} (1-t)\cdot 0 + 0\cdot (-1) = 0\amp\quad 0\lt  t \lt 1\\ 0\cdot 1 + (-t+1)\cdot 0 = 0\amp\quad 1\lt t \lt 2\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C_2} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_2} \vec F\ d\vec r =\int_a^b \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt +  \int_b^c \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt= \int_0^{1} 0 \ dt + \int_{1}^2 0 \ dt  = 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the vector field <m>\vec{F}=\langle -y,x\rangle</m> and the
        curves
        <m>C_{1}: \vec{r}_{1}(t)= \langle\cos (t), \sin (t)\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq \pi </m>,
        and <m>C_{2}: \vec{r}_{2}(t)=\langle 1-2t, 0\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq 1</m>.
        Note that both curves start at <m>(1,0)</m> and end at
        <m>(-1,0)</m>. Verify that
        <m>\int_{C_{1}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \neq \int_{C_{2}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-4">
        <caption>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first and second quadrant, and the line segment starting from (1,0) and ending at (-1,0). On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a counter-clockwise swirl.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 23.4. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-23.4.png">
              <shortdescription>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first and second quadrant, and the line segment starting from (1,0) and ending at (-1,0). On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a counter-clockwise swirl.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first and second quadrant, and the line segment starting from <m>(1,0)</m> and ending at <m>(-1,0)</m>. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a counter-clockwise swirl.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>C_1 : \vec r (t) = \langle \cos t , \sin t \rangle , \  0\leq t \leq \pi , \quad \vec r ' (t) = \langle -\sin t , \cos t \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) = \langle -\sin t,  \cos t \rangle , \quad \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) = \sin^2 t + \cos^2 t = 1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C_1} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_1} \vec F\ d\vec r =\int_a^b \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt = \int_0^{\pi} 1 \ dt  =\pi</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>C_2 : \vec l (t) = \langle 1-2t,0 \cos t \rangle , \  0\leq t  1, \quad \vec r ' (t) = \langle  -2 ,0 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) = \langle 0,1-2 t \rangle , \quad \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) = -2\cdot 0 + 0\cdot (1-2t) = 0</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C_2} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_2} \vec F\ d\vec r =\int_a^b \vec F ( \vec r (t)) \vec r' (t)\ dt = \int_0^{1}  0\ dt  =0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Two things to note from <xref ref="example-ch05-01-ex-23-3"/> and
      <xref ref="example-ch05-01-ex-23-4"/> that are true in general:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a curve <m>C</m> in the domain of a vector field
            <m>\vec{F}</m>,
            <md>
              \int_{-C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=-\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Given a vector field <m>\vec{F}</m>, two points <m>P \neq Q</m>
            in its domain, and two different curves <m>C_{1}</m> and
            <m>C_{2}</m> connecting <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>, in most cases,
            <md>
              \int_{C_{1}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \neq \int_{C_{2}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In general, different curves connecting two points give
                  different values for line integrals.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Direction Interpretation</title>

    <p>
      Given a vector field <m>\vec{F}</m>, a curve <m>C</m> parametrized as
      <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>, and a point ( <m>x_{k}, y_{k}</m> ) on the curve,
      since <m>\vec{F}_{k} \cdot \vec{T}_{k}</m> can be positive or negative
      (depending on the angle between the vectors), the line integral can
      be positive or negative.
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence, we can sometimes determine whether
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}</m> is positive or negative by
      looking at the angles between the vectors:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If for most of the curve, the angle <m>\theta</m> between
            <m>\vec{F}</m> and <m>\vec{T}</m> satisfies
            <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, then we expect
            <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If for most of the curve, the angle <m>\theta</m> between
            <m>\vec{F}</m> and <m>\vec{T}</m> satisfies
            <m>\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \leq \pi</m>, then we expect
            <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \lt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            It is also possible for <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=0</m>
            (for example, if <m>\vec{F} \cdot \vec{T}=0</m> for all points on
            <m>C)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the vector field and curves plotted below. Use these plots
        to determine the sign of <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}</m>
        along each curve.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-5">
        <caption>On the background a circle centred at the origin and traversed clockwise and in a different colour a line segment on the positive part of x -axis, traversed from left to right. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-09. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-09.png">
              <shortdescription>On the background a circle centred at the origin and traversed clockwise and in a different colour a line segment on the positive part of x -axis, traversed from left to right. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the background a circle centred at the origin and traversed clockwise and in a different colour a line segment on the positive part of <m>x</m>-axis, traversed from left to right. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors create a clockwise swirl and their magnitude increases away from the origin.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>C_1</m> be the circle as traversed above and <m>C_2</m> be
        the line segment as traversed above. Notice that at any given point
        the tangent vector of <m>C_1</m> will match the vectors of the
        field. Hence one can infer that
        <m>\int_{C_1} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} =\int_{C_1}\vec F \cdot \vec T\  ds \gt 0 </m>.
        For <m>C_2</m> notice that its tangent vector is perpendicular to
        the vectors of the field. Since the integral
        <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec T\ d s</m>  represents the integral
        of a dot product between the vector field and the tangent vector of
        the parametrization of the curve, given that those are
        perpendicular, one can infer that
        <m>\int_{C_2} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=\int_{C_2}=\vec F \cdot \vec T \  ds  = 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When a force (given by the vector field vectors) acts on an
      object/particle moving along a curve (a particle/curve system), we
      say the vector field is doing work on the system.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Vector Field Line Integrals as Work</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{F}</m> be a continuous force field and let <m>C</m> be a
        smooth oriented curve in its domain. The work done by <m>\vec{F}</m>
        on the system given by a particle moving along <m>C</m> is
        <md>
          W=\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the work done by the force field
        <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle x,z,-y\right\rangle</m> acting on the
        curve/particle system given by
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle t^2, t^{3}, t\right\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq 1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-6">
        <caption>On the foreground 3-dimensional vector field with vectors swirling about the x-axis, on the background the curve traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = t squared, y = t cubed, z = t, for t from 0 to 1.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-11. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-6" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-11.png">
              <shortdescription>On the foreground 3-dimensional vector field with vectors swirling about the x-axis, on the background the curve traced by the vector r of t with coordinates x = t squared, y = t cubed, z = t, for t from 0 to 1.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the foreground 3-dimensional vector field with vectors swirling about the <m>x</m>-axis, on the background the curve traced by the vector <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle t^2, t^{3}, t\right\rangle</m>, for <m>t</m> from 0 to 1.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec r(t)=\left\langle t^2, t^{3}, t\right\rangle \Rightarrow x(t) = t^2, \ y(t) = t^3, \ z(t) = t,\ 0\leq t \leq 1 \Rightarrow \vec F (\vec r(t)) = \langle t^2 , t , -t^3 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r' (t) = \langle 2t , 3t^2 , 1 \rangle, \Rightarrow  \vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec r' (t) = t^2\cdot 2t + t\cdot 3t^2 +-t^3 \cdot 1 = 4t^3</mrow>
          <mrow>\mathbf W = \int_C \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_0^1  \vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec r' (t) \ dt =\int_0^1 4t^3\ dt = \left . t^4 \right |_0^1 =1</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Work Interpretation</title>

    <p>
      Under the work interpretation, it is important to understand what
      positive, negative, and zero work mean:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \gt 0</m>, energy is being
            gained by the curve/particle system (in the form of kinetic and
            potential energy).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \lt 0</m>, energy is being
            lost by the curve/particle system (in the form of heat,
            friction, etc.).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=0</m>, this does not mean
            the vector field forces didn't affect the system, more
            generally, the amount of energy gained was the same as the
            energy lost.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>Recall: A curve <m>C</m> is:</p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Smooth: If it has no sharp edges/corners.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Closed: If it is a loop (has no discernible starting/ending
            point).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Simple: If it doesn't cross itself.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      We call a curve <m>C</m> that is simple, closed, and smooth, a SCS
      curve.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Suppose you have a 2-dimensional vector field <m>\vec{F}</m> and a
      SCS curve <m>C</m> in its domain. We define the circulation of
      <m>\vec{F}</m> along <m>C</m> as a measure of how much the vector
      field (vectors) points in the direction of <m>C</m> (the unit tangent
      vectors of <m>C)</m>. That is, we define the circulation of
      <m>\vec{F}</m> along <m>C</m> precisely as
      <md>
        \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
      </md>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-circulation-boat">
      <caption>A boat traversing on a closed simple curve, placed over a clockwise rotating current.</caption>

      <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-13. -->
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-circulation-boat" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-13.png">
            <shortdescription>A boat traversing on a closed simple curve, placed over a clockwise rotating current.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>A boat traversing on a closed simple curve, placed over a clockwise rotating current.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Vector Field Line Integrals as Circulation</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{F}</m> be a 2-dimensional continuous force field and let
        <m>C</m> be a SCS curve in its domain. The circulation of
        <m>\vec{F}</m> along <m>C</m> is computed as
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <title>Circulation Interpretation</title>

    <p>
      If the path <m>C</m> is not closed that we call this
      <alert>Flow</alert>. When we are talking about circulation along a
      boundary, the sign of the integral tells us the following:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \gt 0</m>, most of the
            vector field is in the direction of <m>C</m>. Hence, most
            particles (whose motion is represented by <m>\vec{F}</m> ) are
            moving along <m>C</m> in the same orientation (as <m>C</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \lt 0</m>, most of the
            vector field is in the direction opposite of <m>C</m>. Hence,
            most particles are moving along <m>C</m> in the opposite
            orientation.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=0</m>, there is no
            discernible motion along <m>C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the vector field <m>\vec{F}=\langle x-y, -y\rangle</m> and the
        curve <m>C</m> parametrized by
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle\sin (t), \cos (t)\rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>, determine whether most of the vector
        field is in the direction of <m>C</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-7">
        <caption>on the background the unit circle centred at the origin, on the foreground a vector field whose vectors point away from the line y equals x and towards the x-axis, with increasing magnitude away from the origin.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-15. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-7" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-15.png">
              <shortdescription>on the background the unit circle centred at the origin, on the foreground a vector field whose vectors point away from the line y equals x and towards the x-axis, with increasing magnitude away from the origin.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>on the background the unit circle centred at the origin, on the foreground a vector field whose vectors point away from the line <m>y=x</m> and towards the <m>x</m>-axis, with increasing magnitude away from the origin.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec r(t)=\langle\sin (t), \cos (t)\rangle \Rightarrow  \vec F (\vec r(t)) = \langle \sin (t) - \cos (t), - \cos (t) \rangle, \quad \vec r' (t) = \langle \cos (t) , -\sin  (t)\rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec r' (t) = \sin (t)\cos (t) -\cos^2 (t) + \cos(t)\sin (t) = -\cos^2 (t) + 2\sin (t) \cos (t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_C \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_0^{2\pi}  \vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec r' (t) \ dt =\int_0^{2\pi} - \cos^2 (t) +2\sin (t)\cos (t)\ dt  =</mrow>
          <mrow>-\int_0^{2\pi}  \cos^2 (t) \ dt  +2\int_0^{2\pi}  +\sin (t)\cos (t)\ dt \underset{\substack{t= 0 \Rightarrow u = 0\\ t =2\pi \Rightarrow u =0}}{\overset{\substack{u=\sin t \\ du = \cos t dt}}{=}}  -\int_0^{2\pi}  \cos^2 (t)\ dt +2\int_0^0 u\ du =</mrow>
          <mrow>-\int_0^{2\pi}  \cos^2 (t) dt +0 \lt 0.</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{Hence most of the vector field is in the opposite direction of}C</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Although we will mostly work with SCS curves when talking about
      circulation, we can generalize this idea to curves that are
      piece-wise smooth (a union of smooth curves connected at their
      endpoints).
    </p>

    <p>
      We call a simple, closed, piece-wise smooth curve <m>C</m> a SCPS
      curve.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a line integral (as a sum of
        integrals) that gives the circulation of the vector field
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle 1,x\rangle</m> along the SCPS curve <m>C</m>,
        traversed counter-clockwise, shown below (First quadrant of the
        unit circle, and line segments on the respective axes).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-ex-23-8">
        <caption>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the x and y axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors are fixed on each vertical line. In each of those lines they point from left to right while at the same time their up-down direction is proportional to the x coordinate of the point they originate.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-16. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-ex-23-8" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-16.png">
              <shortdescription>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the x and y axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors are fixed on each vertical line. In each of those lines they point from left to right while at the same time their up-down direction is proportional to the x coordinate of the point they originate.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the background the part of the unit circle belonging on the first quadrant, the line segments starting from the origin to and ending to the 1 of the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> axis respectively. On the foreground a vector field whose vectors are fixed on each vertical line. In each of those lines they point from left to right while at the same time their up-down direction is proportional to the <m>x</m> coordinate of the point they originate.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>C: \vec r (t) = \begin{cases} \text{Line segment of the y axis}\amp\quad 0\leq t \leq 1\\ \text{Line segment of the x axis}\amp\quad 1\leq t \leq 2\\ \text{Unit circle, first quadrant }  \amp\quad 2\leq t \leq 2+\frac{\pi}{2}\\ \end{cases} =</mrow>
          <mrow>\begin{cases} \langle 0,1 \rangle + t \langle 0 , -1 \rangle = \langle 0,1-t \rangle\amp\quad 0\leq t \leq 1\\ \langle 0,0 \rangle + (t-1) \langle 1 , 0 \rangle = \langle 1-t, 0 \rangle\amp\quad 1\leq t \leq 2\\ \langle \cos (t-2), \sin (t-2) \rangle  \amp\quad 2\leq t \leq 2+\frac{\pi}{2}\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r' (t) =   \begin{cases} \langle 0,-1 \rangle\amp\quad 0\lt  t \lt 1\\ \langle 1, 0 \rangle\amp\quad 1\lt t \lt 2\\ \langle -\sin (t-2), \cos (t-2) \rangle  \amp\quad 2 \lt t \lt 2+\frac{\pi}{2}\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t))  =   \begin{cases} \langle 1 , 0\rangle\amp\quad 0\leq  t \leq 1\\ \langle 1, t-1 \rangle\amp\quad 1\leq t \leq 2\\ \langle 1, \cos (t-2) \rangle  \amp\quad 2\leq t \leq 2+\frac{\pi}{2}\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F ( \vec r (t)) \cdot \vec r'(t) =  \begin{cases} 1\cdot 0 + 0\cdot (-1) = 0\amp\quad 0\lt  t \lt 1\\ 1\cdot 1 + (t-1)\cdot 0 = 1\amp\quad 1\lt t \lt 2\\ -\sin (t-2) +\cos^2 (t-2)  \amp\quad 2 \lt t \lt 2+\frac{\pi}{2}\\ \end{cases}</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_{C} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_0^1 0 \ dt + \int_1^2 1 \ dt + \int_2^{2+\frac{\pi}{2}}  -\sin (t-2) +\cos^2 (t-2) \ dt</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      In defining the circulation of a vector field <m>\vec{F}</m> along a
      closed curve <m>C</m>, we were interested in the scalar component of
      the vector field in the direction of <m>C</m> (measured using
      <m>\vec{T}</m> ), which is why we used
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Flux of a Vector Field</title>

    <p>
      For flux, instead of wanting to measure how much of the vector field
      is in the direction of <m>C</m>, we instead want to measure how much
      of the vector field is normal/perpendicular/orthogonal to <m>C</m>.
      Hence, instead of using the scalar component of <m>\vec{F}</m> in the
      direction of a unit tangent vector <m>\vec{T}</m>, we will use the
      scalar component of <m>\vec{F}</m> in the direction of <m>\vec{N}</m>
      (a unit vector normal to <m>C</m> ). Hence, we will define the flux
      of <m>\vec{F}</m> on across <m>C</m> as
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s</m>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-01-flux-normal">
      <caption>On the background a vector field, on the foreground a simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, and vectors placed on it, on a direction perpendicular to its tangent vector.</caption>

      <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-17. -->
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-01-flux-normal" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_11_10_e1bad327dc4b7562db72g-17.png">
            <shortdescription>On the background a vector field, on the foreground a simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, and vectors placed on it, on a direction perpendicular to its tangent vector.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>On the background a vector field, on the foreground a simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, and vectors placed on it, on a direction perpendicular to its tangent vector.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Vector Field Line Integrals as Flux</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> be a
        2 -dimensional continuous vector field and let <m>C</m> be a SCPS
        oriented curve in its domain parametrized by
        <m>\vec{r}(t)= \langle x(t), y(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m> and
        oriented counter clockwise. Let
        <m>\vec n(t) = \langle y'(t), -x'(t)  \rangle</m>, a normal vector
        that points to the right of the direction that the curve is
        parametrized with, and <m>\vec N</m> the unit normal vector in its
        direction. The flux of <m>\vec{F}</m> across <m>C</m> is computed
        as
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s= \int_{a}^b \vec{F}(\vec r (t)) \cdot \vec n(t) d t=\int_{a}^{b}\left(f(x(t), y(t)) y^{\prime}(t)-g(x(t), y(t)) x^{\prime}(t)\right) d t
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When we are talking about flux across a boundary, the sign of the
      integral tells us the following:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s \gt 0</m>, most of the
            vector field points away from <m>C</m>. Hence, most particles
            (whose motion is represented by <m>\vec{F}</m> ) are moving from
            inside <m>R</m> towards the outside.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s \lt 0</m>, most of the
            vector field points towards (the inside of) <m>C</m>. Hence,
            most particles are moving from outside <m>R</m> towards the
            inside.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=0</m>, the same amount
            of particles (could be none) are entering and exiting the
            region <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether most particles, whose motion is represented by
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle  x+y, x\rangle</m>, are entering or exiting
        across the ellipse
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle 2 \cos (t),  \sin (t)\rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec r(t)=\langle 2 \cos (t),  \sin (t)\rangle \Rightarrow  \vec F (\vec r(t)) = \langle 2\cos (t) +\sin (t),\cos (t) \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r' (t) = \langle  -2\sin  (t),\cos (t)\rangle \overset{\vec n (r) = \langle y' (t), - x'(t)\rangle}{\Rightarrow} \vec n (t) = \langle \cos (t) , 2 \sin (t) \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec n (t) = (2\cos (t) +\sin (t))\cos (t) +2\cos (t) 2\sin (t) = 2\cos^2 (t) + 5\cos(t)\sin (t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_C \vec F \cdot \vec N \ ds = \int_0^{2\pi}  \vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec n (t) \ dt =\int_0^{2\pi} 2 \cos^2 (t) +5\sin (t)\cos (t)\ dt  =</mrow>
          <mrow>2\int_0^{2\pi}  \cos^2 (t) \ dt  +5\int_0^{2\pi}  +\sin (t)\cos (t)\ dt \underset{\substack{t= 0 \Rightarrow u = 0\\ t =2\pi \Rightarrow u =0}}{\overset{\substack{u=\sin t \\ du = \cos t dt}}{=}}  2\int_0^{2\pi}  \cos^2 (t)\ dt +5\cancelto{0}{\int_0^0 u\ du} =</mrow>
          <mrow>2\left ( \cancelto{0}{\left .\frac{1}{2}\cos (t)\sin (t) \right |_0^{2\pi}} +  \frac{1}{2}\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^0 (t)\ dt\right ) = 2\cdot\frac{1}{2} \int_0^{2\pi}\ dt = 2\pi \gt 0 .</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{ Hence most of the particles are exiting the ellipse, since it is parametrized counter-clockwise.}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-01-ex-23-10">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether most particles, whose motion is represented by
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle  y, x\rangle</m>, are entering or exiting across
        the ellipse <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle  \cos (t),  \sin (t)\rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec r(t)=\langle  \cos (t),  \sin (t)\rangle \Rightarrow  \vec F (\vec r(t)) = \langle \sin (t),\cos (t) \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r' (t) = \langle  -\sin  (t),\cos (t)\rangle \overset{\vec n (r) = \langle y' (t), - x'(t)\rangle}{\Rightarrow} \vec n (t) = \langle \cos (t) ,  \sin (t) \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec n (t) = \sin (t)\cos (t) +\cos (t) \sin (t) = 2\cos(t)\sin (t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_C \vec F \cdot \vec N \ ds = \int_0^{2\pi}  \vec F (\vec r(t))\cdot \vec n (t) \ dt =\int_0^{2\pi} 2 \sin (t)\cos (t)\ dt   \underset{\substack{t= 0 \Rightarrow u = 0\\ t =2\pi \Rightarrow u =0}}{\overset{\substack{u=\sin t \\ du = \cos t dt}}{=}}</mrow>
          <mrow>2\cancelto{0}{\int_0^0 u\ du} =0</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{ Hence the same amount of particles are exiting and entering the circle.}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Summary of Interpretations</title>

    <p>
      For the line integral <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> :
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Directional interpretation.</p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If for most of the curve, the angle <m>\theta</m> between
                  <m>\vec{F}</m> and <m>\vec{T}</m> satisfies
                  <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, then we expect
                  <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \gt 0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If for most of the curve, the angle <m>\theta</m> between
                  <m>\vec{F}</m> and <m>\vec{T}</m> satisfies
                  <m>\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \leq \pi</m>, then we expect
                  <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \lt 0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  It is also possible for
                  <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=0</m> (for example, if
                  <m>\vec{F} \cdot \vec{T}=0</m> for all points on <m>C</m>
                  ).
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>Work interpretation.</p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \gt 0</m>, energy
                  is being gained by the curve/particle system (in the form
                  of kinetic and potential energy).
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r} \lt 0</m>, energy
                  is being lost by the curve/particle system (in the form of
                  heat, friction, etc.).
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=0</m>, this does
                  not mean the vector field forces didn't affect the system,
                  more generally, the amount of energy gained was the same
                  as the energy lost.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Circulation Interpretation (if <m>C</m> is simple, closed,
            piece-wise smooth):
          </p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \gt 0</m>, most
                  of the vector field is in the direction of <m>C</m>.
                  Hence, most particles (whose motion is represented by
                  <m>\vec{F}</m> ) are moving along <m>C</m> in the same
                  orientation (as <m>C</m> ).
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \lt 0</m>, most
                  of the vector field is in the direction opposite of
                  <m>C</m>. Hence, most particles are moving along <m>C</m>
                  in the opposite orientation.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=0</m>, there is
                  no discernible motion along <m>C</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      For the integral <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{n} d s</m>, when
      <m>C</m> is a simple, closed, piece-wise smooth curve enclosing a
      region <m>R</m> , oriented counter-clockwise:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Flux interpretation :</p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s \gt 0</m>, most
                  of the vector field points away from <m>C</m>. Hence, most
                  particles (whose motion is represented by <m>\vec{F}</m> )
                  are moving from inside <m>R</m> towards the outside.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s \lt 0</m>, most
                  of the vector field points towards (the inside of)
                  <m>C</m>. Hence, most particles are moving from outside
                  <m>R</m> towards the inside.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=0</m>, the same
                  amount of particles (could be none) are entering and
                  exiting the region <m>R</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

</section>
